    "summary": "Alligator jam fuels fire‑filled second set with stellar drums",
    "blurb": "The second set erupts with a smoking Alligator jam that rolls into a tight Dancin' In The Street and a punchy Not Fade Away. Jerry’s searing guitar and the dual‑drum thunder keep the energy high, while Pigpen’s organ adds a bluesy plug‑in moment. Highlights include Morning Dew, New Orleans and an hour‑long Turn On Your Lovelight sequence.",
    "review": "On June 6, 1970 at the Fillmore West the Dead delivered a performance that still feels fresh after more than five decades. After a modest acoustic first set, the band launched into a second set that became a masterclass in jamcraft: a smoking Alligator jam led straight into a tight Dancin' In The Street and then exploded on Not Fade Away. Jerry Garcia’s lead was on fire – his searing solo over Alligator and soaring lines on Morning Dew earned unanimous praise, while the twin‑drum attack from Mickey Hart and Bill Kreutzmann drove the extended Turn On Your Lovelight/Not Fade Away sequence with thunderous precision. Pigpen’s organ moment added a Janis‑Joplin‑esque blues bite, and the groove stayed tight through New Orleans, Attics Of My Life and the later Dark‑ness jam. The show caps off with an encore of Uncle John’s Band, but it’s the second‑set marathon that makes this Fillmore West night a must‑listen for any Deadhead.",
